.endsect
.newsect Introduction
This document covers installation of the ${shname} system.
Most of the document is a tutorial based around building
a sample class.
This tutorial should help outline the daily care and feeding of ${shname}.
If your site has
.program make,
then tracking changes in the file can be automated.
.!
  The ${shanme} uses several resources from the host.
Normal utilties to produce executables from "C" source code
are required.
The UNIX user of the ${shname} asks the system to supply
.program cpp
(the C pre-processor).
Included with the ${shname} is DECUS's version of
.program cpp
in case there is none locally (like most AmigaDOS and MS-DOS systems).
.!
  The ${shname} must somehow find the include files.
Environment variables hold the list of directories to search.
Normally one more directory must be appended to a list somewhere.
Alternatively, a script or
.filename makefile
make explicitly specify a directory (see man page for
.program ooc
).
Include files from ${lgname} don't need to mingle with
your systems own files, if you don't want.
.!
   Linking does require searching through libraries.
By installing the libraries in
.filename /usr/lib
the class libraries can be seperate from the systems libraries.
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 System Components
   A running ${shname} application is the product of
source files, interface definitions, and translation programs.
The ${lgname} programs include an ${shname} to "C" source code translator,
a class interface helper,
and a pre-linker utility.
Your site must provide a K&R version 1 [Kern78] "C" compiler.
.!
  The interface definition file includes information about the
class, its superclass, and method return types.
The prelinker program arranges for the needed classes to be included
from the ${shname} object library.
The output "C" file is then linked along with application files
and the ${shname} libraries.
.!
.!
.save
.center on
.nofill
.nojustify
