.endsect
.newsect Installation
From the overview document, you know that ${shname} reads
a class file, imports public interface files for classes
you reference, and then outputs a "C" file ready for your
"C" compiler.
Where these things come from and where the go are
determined by environment variables.
For the Amiga system, Manx's
.program set
is used, not the ENV: directory (sorry).
For UNIX users, use the normal shell environment variables.
.!
  To get the system up and ready for class development
requires a sub-directory with a few directories.
You may elect to not mix ${shname} header and library files
with the rest of your system.
Here is a basic roadmap for the directories:
.br
.figopen 5 12
  ---> OOC -----+----- methods
                |
                +----- include ---+--- objc
                |                 |
                |                 +--- apps
                |                 |
                |                 +--- exec (for UNIX only)
                |
                |
(from here on, these directories may mingle..)
                |
                +----- lib
                |
                +----- bin

.figclose
.figure ${shname} Directory Tree

Into the 'methods' directory you should copy all the *.m files
from this directory on the distribution disk.
These serve as good examples of how to call various services.
.!
  The 'include' directory contains the public interface header
files for classes in your ${shname} library.
Copy this directory and its subdirectories verbatim from the
distribution disk.
For UNIX people, the
.filename exec
directory contains files defining
the list and node primitive structures used internally to
the ${shname} translator and the application substrate.
Assign the variable INCLUDE to also specify this directory.
.!
  The remaining directories can be seperate from your system
directories if you don't want "mixing" of programs.
A temporary directory may be specified with the variable CCTEMP.
If not specified, a default directory is used, depending on the
host system (AmigaDOS, UNIX or MS-DOS).
.!
.!
.! -------------------------------------------------------
.!
.endsect
.newsect Daily Usage
.!
**** THIS DOCUMENT IS UNFINISHED ****

Now that you got the thing, it's time to use the thing.
The thing, here, refers to the ${lgname} system:
translator, utilities, and class libraries.
At present (April 1990), the system is remarkably independent of
the actual machine it's running on.
Applications can be generated with 16-bit or 32-bit int's.
The translator relies strictly on the "C" runtime library,
not any Amiga or U*NX specific routines.
So developing an application should be just like developing any
other "C" language application.
Well, it's almost the same...
.!
   A few rules for generating an application will be detailed below.
These refer to source and
.program make
rules.
In most circumstances, plain "C" files may be added coagulated into
your application.  These files, however, won't be taking advantage
of any special ${oo} features.
By adding one translator directive (@includes), a source file
may onw open up the power of this exciting programming system.
The system also requires additional libraries at link time.
.!
.!
.! -------------------------------------------------------
.!
.endsect
.newsect Application Program Concept
.!
**** THIS DOCUMENT IS UNFINISHED ****

In the ${oo} world, the programmer dreams of objects
that actions affect.
Objects are usually modeled after real life entities.
"Look for structure, other systems, devices, things remembered,
roles played, locations, organization units,
and descriptive catalogs." [Coad89]
"As a system evolves, its tasks may change drastically.
Much more persistency may be found in the classes of data
it manipulates.." [Meyer88]
These objects may maintain state internally, or relie
on other objects to remember.
.!
  Objects are instances of a class, and all objects of
a given class react similarily (although, based on state, reactions
may be different).
Each class will be a problem solving module.
"Modular concepts apply to programming as well as specification
and design." [Meyer88].
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Sample System: Queue
  This paper will outline the development of the
.class Queue
class.
This queue is single ended, circular, and will expand to meet its
own size requirements.
This class basically joins
.class OrderedCollection's
input and adding abilities to
.class Sequence's
output and enumeration methods.
It will inherit from the abstract class
.class Collection
directly; this should keep minimal any awkwardness trying to shoehorn the
.class Queue
class on top of another class.
You should have a copy of the
.class Collection
class method file (.m) on handy during your reading here.
.!
.endsect
.! -------------------------------------------------------
.!
.newsect Class File
.!
**** THIS DOCUMENT IS UNFINISHED ****

To create a class requires the creation of two files:
the interface and the implementation.
The interface file is a public header that all clients of the
class will read.
The implementation is where the code to support the class is
located.
This file will be described first.
.br
.!
.sect 1 1 Methods
  Time to invoke your favorite program editor and start writing.
Normally you would invoke your favorite pencil and start writing
on paper.
Planning ahead on which methods
a class will implemenet is very important, as they will dictate what
type of services the class will be providing,
and hence, what data structures will be used.
For the
.class Queue,
we'll be adding to the front and removing from the tail.
Any absolute references will have to be adjusted
to fit around the circular-ness of the Queue.
Only some of the conversion methods (ie, asBag) will be implemented here,
but you may flesh out the class completely.
.!
  Examining the
.class Collection
method file, you'll notice the add and remove methods are very generic.
These will be interpreted as "addHead:" and "remTail:"

The
.program prelinker
builds a list of modules provided by the user.
When the runtime system initializes itself, it walks down this
list finding all the dependent classes.
From this list, all the methods are given codes, and every class
initialized.
.!
.!
.sect 1 2 After Translation
.!
  A prelinker is used to build a list of classes
that will be brought together to form the final application.
A small
.filename .c
file is built, which must be compiled and added into
the list of object modules for the appliction.
This list tells the linker which classes to extract from
the library, including any dependencies the included classes
need, like other classes.
It's also used by the substrate at startup-time to build
a central list of method selector codes and class names.
Normally, this small file need only be rebuilt when the
list of modules the user provides changes in some way,
or a new class is added to the application.
.!
.!
.endsect
.! -------------------------------------------------------
.!
.newsect Translation
.!
.!
The first two passes are driven by ${shname}, which calls
.program cpp
first.
The translator then converts class definitions and methods
into legal "C" code.
The translator uses information in the @interface clauses
to relate the external classes.
The system's "C" compiler runs next.
The prelinker creates a list of classes for use by the substrate.
.!
.!
.! -------------------------------------------------------
.!
.endsect
.newsect Theory of Methods and Factories
.!
This section describes the run-time environment the objects exist within.
It describes the theory behind the
"reality" the application runs inside of.
.!
.figname cim +1
.!
.figopen 0 30
.echar ~
.bold
 Objects             Instance Base             Factory Base
                                         ,_________________________,
                                         |                         |
               ,-----------------------------------------------,   |
               |                         |                     |   |
+--------+     V    +--------------+     V   +--------------+  |   |
| isa   ----------> | isa         ---------> | isa         ------->|
+========+     ^    +--------------+     ^   +--------------+  |   |
               |    | super    --> Nil   |   | super       ----+   |
               |    | "Object"     |     |   | "_Object"    |      |
               |    | size       4 |     |   | size      24 |      |
               |    +--------------+     |   +--------------+      |
               |                         |                         |
               \----------------------,  \---------------------,   |
                                      |                        |   |
+--------+          +--------------+  |      +--------------+  |   |
| isa   ----------> | isa         ---------> | isa         ------->|
+--------+     ^    +--------------+  |   ^  +--------------+  |   |
|        |     |    | super       ----'   |  | super       ----'   |
+--------+     |    | "View"       |      |  | "_View"      |      |
               |    | size      20 |      |  | size      24 |      |
               |    +--------------+      |  +--------------+      |
               |                          |                        |
               \----------------------,   \--------------------,   |
                                      |                        |   |
+--------+          +--------------+  |      +--------------+  |   |
| isa   ----------> | isa         ---------> | isa         ------->|
|        |          +--------------+  |      +--------------+  |
+--------+          | super       ----'      | super       ----'
|        |          | "Fixture"    |         | "_Fixture"   |
+--------+          | size      26 |         | size      24 | 
                    +--------------+         +--------------+

.echar _
.figclose
.figure Classes in Memory
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Factories
  Software factories create software objects.
These objects carry inbedded information about how much space
they occupy and what kind of method selectors they respond to.
In a factory method, "self" refers to the factory object.
It is a real variable (along with "__cmd" which is the method selector)
declared as part of the method definition clause.
"self" can be reassigned to a new instance of itself and
returned from a factory method, just as in [Cox86].
However, this practice is discouraged by the author.
Factory objects can never be free'd.
.!
.!
.sect 1 2 Instances
  Once created ([new] method),
objects can be manipulated and assigned at will.
The caller must track memory usage since there is
no ${agc} nor referencing counting.
This does place an extra burden on the programmer, but all of us
learned Pascal in order to better discipline ourselves...
.!
.!
.sect 1 3 Methods
.!

.endsect
.!
.! --------------------------------------------------------
.!
.newsect Interpreting Runtime Errors
.!
Because of the dynamic nature of messages and objects,
only a limitied amount of checking can be done at compile time.
If this system, all checking of whether a message is appropriate
for the object is done at runtime.
If something is amiss, the application can abort.
.!
  When the first thing your program does is give the "message selector 0"
error, suspect your
.filename makefile.
If the prelinker isn't told about your
.filename main.m,
then its method won't be resolved (they'll remian 0).
The program will start up, but abort with the first message
performed from your main file.
.!
  If you send a message to a nil ptr, the message dispatcher
will just ignore your attempt.
If the object has been free'd, but you have a dangling pointer,
you'll also be ignore.
There are compile time switches that can provide some
diagnostic messages in these instances, but usually they
are a nuicence to have around.
.!
  If you send a message that an object can handle, you'll
get an error message like:
.opencode
(oc_error) Slider does not recognize [moreGadgets:]!
.endcode
This means an object of the class
.class Slider
could not handle the method "moreGadgets:",
and neither could any of its parent classes.
If the class name had been
.class __Slider,
then a factory method was being invoked.
.!
.!
.endsect
.! --------------------------------------------------------
.!
.newsect The Reality of Runtime
.!
Before the user application can begin running,
the system must initialize itself.
Each class will see the "initialize" message first,
which will most likely be fielded by class
.class Object
as the default implementation.
The user's main() function name has been changed via a #define in 
.filename <objc/objc.h>.
This allows (almost) seamless operation
with pre-existing source files, and requires nothing special from
the run-time environment.
Since many microcomputer "C" runtime libraries don't have __atexit(),
the include file also redirects exit().
This allows the substrate to cleanup.
Setting the global (BOOL) _msgTrace will print out some statistics
when the application exits.
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Method Selector Codes
 jkjk
.!
.endsect
.!
.! --------------------------------------------------------
